%I A399605 #14 Nov 02 2026 17:09:43
%S A399605 1,1,2,3,5,9,13,18,24,30,38,46,56
%N A399605 Number of distinct values taken by the number of intersection points of n lines in the plane, no two parallel.
%C A399605 The number of intersection points of n lines, no two parallel, lies between 1 and binomial(n,2) (for n = 1 it is 0); a(n) is the number of values that occur. Dually, a(n) is the number of distinct numbers of lines determined by n points in the plane (Grünbaum's problem). Erdős (1972) and Salamon and Erdős (1988) determined the set of these values for all sufficiently large n.
%C A399605 The values binomial(n,2) - 1 and binomial(n,2) - 3 never occur. For n >= 10 no value strictly between n and 2n-4 occurs (Elliott). For n >= 13 the values below 2n-1 are exactly 1, n, 2n-4 and 2n-2. In particular 2n-3 intersection points are impossible for n >= 12: for n = 12 by exhaustive computation, for n >= 13 by a computer-assisted counting argument based on the inequalities of Melchior and Hirzebruch.
%C A399605 a(n) = binomial(n,2) - 2n + 4 for n = 12 and n = 13, and 65 <= a(14) <= 67. The formula does not hold for all n: at n = 17 the value 2n-1 = 33 is also impossible.
%C A399605 The multisets of numbers of lines through the intersection points are counted by A399464.
%C A399605 Differs from A129230: a(n) = A129230(n-2) for 4 <= n <= 12, but A129230(11) = 55 while a(13) = 56.
%D A399605 P. D. T. A. Elliott, On the number of circles determined by n points, Acta Math. Acad. Sci. Hungar. 18 (1967), 181-188.
%D A399605 P. Erdős, On a problem of Grünbaum, Canad. Math. Bull. 15 (1972), 23-25.
%D A399605 B. Grünbaum, Arrangements and Spreads, CBMS Regional Conference Series in Mathematics 10, AMS, Providence, 1972.
%D A399605 F. Hirzebruch, Arrangements of lines and algebraic surfaces, in: Arithmetic and Geometry, Vol. II, Progr. Math. 36, Birkhäuser, Boston, 1983, 113-140.
%D A399605 L. M. Kelly and W. O. J. Moser, On the number of ordinary lines determined by n points, Canad. J. Math. 10 (1958), 210-219.
%D A399605 P. Salamon and P. Erdős, The solution to a problem of Grünbaum, Canad. Math. Bull. 31 (1988), 129-138.
%H A399605 Petra Radko, <a href="/A399605/a399605.txt">Table of the attainable numbers of intersection points for n <= 13</a>
%H A399605 Petra Radko, <a href="https://gwynerva.github.io/line-pencils/sequences/points/">How n Lines Can Meet: numbers of intersection points</a>, 2026.
%H A399605 Petra Radko, <a href="https://github.com/Gwynerva/line-pencils">Programs, certificates, logs and verification notes</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%e A399605 For n = 4 the possible numbers of intersection points are 1, 4 and 6, so a(4) = 3. Four lines not all through one point have at least four intersection points, and 5 is impossible: three lines through one point merge three intersection points into one, which reduces the count from 6 to 4.
%e A399605 For n = 5 the possible numbers are 1, 5, 6, 8 and 10, so a(5) = 5.
%Y A399605 Cf. A072964, A003035, A129230, A399464, A400149.
